  "worked_solution": "We have the linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a=5,$ $b=9,$ and $c=8.$ We're given that the equation has solutions.\n\nNotice that our equation can't be solved for either of the two variables using integer coefficients. So, let's find the linear representation of $\\text{gcd}(5,9)$ using the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}9 & = & 5\\cdot 1 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 5 & = & 4\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =9-5\\cdot 1 \\\\ 1 & =5-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =5-4 \\\\  & =5-(9-5) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2+9\\cdot (-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ and\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 2+9\\cdot (-1)=1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this equation by $8,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot 2+9\\cdot (-1) & =1 \\\\ 5\\cdot 2\\cdot 8+9\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 8 & =8 \\\\ 5\\cdot 16+9\\cdot (-8) & =8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $(x,y)=(16,-8)$ is a solution to our Diophantine equation.\n\nNow, the general solution is\n\n$$\n(16+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,-8-\\frac{a}{d}t)=(16+9t,\\,-8-5t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$\n\nThe smallest possible positive value of $x=16+9t$ is attained when $t=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{∼}{x}=16+9\\cdot (-1)=7.\n$$",
